From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:57:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: size the geometry heap from measurement

The per-device geometry heap was a fixed 128 MiB placement heap, taken whole
the first time a command buffer draws through it. The size was a placeholder,
not a bound, and a Venus host pays all of it once per guest VkDevice, so the
cost scales with however many devices a guest keeps alive.

Measured instead. The heaviest single command buffer across the CTS
tessellation, geometry, draw and rasterization groups asks the heap for
1.53 MiB. One mebibyte fails those groups and two passes them, so the floor
sits between the two. Sixteen leaves an order of magnitude over the measured
peak and ran a full released mustpass fraction clean.

Demand is linear in patches and primitives, which the API does not bound, so no
fixed size is safe for every application. MESA_KK_HEAP_SIZE_MIB raises it
without a rebuild.

The override only takes 1 to 4095 MiB, the range the GPU allocator's 32-bit
bound can describe, and any other value keeps sixteen: a count that wrapped
the byte size to zero, one Metal refuses and one past 4 GiB used to crash the
first draw that needed the heap or leave the GPU a bound larger than the heap.
A device Metal refuses its heap keeps none, and a draw that needs it fails its
command buffer's recording and records nothing instead of dereferencing it.
A test draws a triangle fan, which is unrolled through the heap, under each
kind of bad value and under a valid one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
